  "question": {
    "text": "Alkyl halide is converted into alkyl isocyanide by reaction with",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "z2D3b",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCN</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "nD5lq",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>CN</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "8LyYD",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCN</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "aP1wF",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgCN</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgCN</mi></math> is covalent in nature so it does not dissociate in to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>CN</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> and on nitrogen electron density is high,and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide or alkyl isonitrile as the main product.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>AgCN</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>RNC</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>AgX</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCN</mi></math> is ionic in nature, so it dissociates completely.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>KCN</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>RCN</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>KX</mi></math></p><p>Hence the answer is D.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
